 ],
 "literal": "In the grove, like the curled tender shoot of a banana tree, / because of caressing its precious head which has sacred power, having lost its strength, / the infatuated male elephant that experienced confusing grief, / while its gentle female elephant with languishing life caresses its weary back, / on the mountain slope where wild cows descend, with difficulty falls asleep. / The intimacy with the lord of such a great mountain land, / has indeed slipped from our hands, though it is one that gives love.",
 "translation": "Like the curled tender shoot of a grove's banana tree, the male elephant, having lost its strength when its precious, sacred head was caressed, suffers from confusing grief. As its gentle mate, her life languishing, strokes its weary back, it finally falls into a difficult sleep on the mountain slopes where wild cows descend. The intimacy of the lord of such great mountains, though it brings us love, has now slipped from our hands.",
 "modern": "சோலையிலுள்ள வாழையின் சுருண்ட குருத்தைப் போன்ற தன் பெருமைமிக்க தலையைத் தடவிக் கொடுத்ததால், தன் வலிமையை இழந்து, மயக்கத்தைத் தரும் துயரத்தை அடைந்த ஆண் யானை, தன் வருந்துகின்ற பெண் யானை தன் மெல்லிய உடலால் தன் களைப்படைந்த முதுகைத் தடவிக் கொடுக்க, காட்டுப் பசுக்கள் இறங்கி வரும் மலைச் சரிவில் அரிதாகக் கண்மூடித் தூங்கும். அத்தகைய பெரிய மலைநாட்டை உடைய தலைவனின் நட்பு, நமக்கு இன்பத்தைத் தருவதாக இருந்தாலும், இப்போது நம் கையை விட்டு நழுவிப் போய்விட்டது.",
 "explanation": "The friend (thozhi) speaks to the heroine (or to the hero within earshot) about the state of their love. She describes a scene on the mountain slopes where a powerful male elephant, exhausted and distressed, is comforted by its loving mate's gentle touch until it falls asleep. This image of tender, protective love in the Kuriñci landscape mirrors the heroine's devotion and her need for the hero's comforting presence. The friend laments that the hero's intimacy, which once brought joy, has now become a source of slipping hope as he delays their formal union. Key words include வேழம் (male elephant), பிடி (female elephant), சிலம்பு (mountain slope), and கைதாழ்ந்தன்று (has slipped from hand).",
